extends Node3D
## The presentation layer (ARCHITECTURE.md §5) for the data-driven production
## chain `sim_core::World` builds internally — rendered per docs/UI_UX.md:
## full 3D voxel models at the §2 32x32x32 tile scale, viewed through a
## FIXED orthographic isometric camera (§1), lit in real time with shadows,
## ambient occlusion and glow (§4), in a grounded industrial-factory palette
## (§3/§7).
##
## Milestone 5 (Economy MVP, ARCHITECTURE.md §9 item 5) added a minimal money
## + contract-status HUD readout, read every frame from `economy_snapshot_json`.
## It renders as UI_UX.md §5's diegetic clipboard — a paper-toned panel with
## checkbox-style tick marks for delivery progress, its own distinct
## PanelContainer rather than folded into the generic dark HUD panel (see
## `_build_contract_panel`). Since contracts became `data/contracts.ron`
## content it shows ALL of them at once, one docket card each on the one
## board, because GAMEPLAY.md §6's diversification pressure only reaches the
## player if every concurrent obligation is visible together.
##
## What has NOT changed from the previous flat-2D version, deliberately: this
## script still learns the chain's shape entirely from `chain_layout_json`
## (static, read once) plus `chunk_snapshot_json`/`dirty_chunk_ids` (dynamic,
## per tick). Stage count, tile positions and item identities all come from
## the simulation; nothing about the chain is hardcoded here, and nothing
## here reads a belt's gap array or a machine's raw `TimedProcess`. Godot
## still draws what the sim says exists — it just draws it far better.
##
## Milestone 6 (Fluids) adds the pipe run: pipe tiles and the liquid inside
## them are built and sized entirely from `fluid_snapshot_json` (see
## `_build_fluid_network` for why that call rather than the chunk snapshots),
## and a pipe answers the same hover tooltip a machine does.
##
## The "Machines improvement" milestone's ticket 4 is the presentation half
## of tickets 1-3. Machines are no longer one generic model on one tile: the
## kind and the tile FOOTPRINT the simulation reports pick the model and size
## its placement (see MACHINE_MODELS / `_footprint_centre`), so the 2x2
## cooking vat genuinely covers its four tiles; the chain's hopper is drawn
## as a real building with the product level inside it tracking its live fill
## (`_update_hopper`, built the same way the pipe run's liquid already was);
## and the one hover mechanism resolves any tile of a multi-tile machine, and
## the hopper, alongside the machines and pipes it already covered.
##
## The delivery-dock ticket's presentation half ends the line properly. The
## chain used to be emptied by a keypress — **C** issued `collect_output`,
## which lifted finished cases straight out of the last machine's buffer —
## and the tile past the packer did not exist. Now the simulation lays one
## more ordinary belt there and stands a `dock` building at its end that
## ships into the economy's shared delivery pool every tick by itself, so
## **C** and its control hint are gone and `_build_dock` draws what replaced
## them. The goods standing on the dock's deck come from
## `economy_snapshot_json`'s `delivery_stock`, NOT from the dock's own buffer,
## which is a pass-through and always reads empty — see DOCK_ASSET.
##
## Assets come from `res://voxels/`, staged there by `cargo xtask` from
## `assets/voxels/models/` (generated by `cargo run -p voxgen`). Each asset is
## one mesh per material class, so steel can be shinier than concrete and
## status lamps can actually emit — UI_UX.md §4's explicit metal exception.
##
## Two view modes, toggled with TAB: ISOMETRIC (this doc's §1 fixed
## orthographic camera, pan/zoom only) and FIRST_PERSON (a free-look
## perspective "walk" camera, not part of UI_UX.md — this project has no
## player entity or collision geometry, so it's a fly-cam: no gravity, no
## walls stop you). Both modes reuse the same single `Camera3D` node,
## switching its projection/position/orientation rather than swapping nodes,
## so "exactly one Camera3D" stays true regardless of mode.

# --- Scale ---------------------------------------------------------------
# UI_UX.md §2 fixes one world tile at 32x32x32 voxels. The .vox importer's
# default Scale option turns one voxel into 0.1 world units, so a tile is
# 3.2 units across and every asset shares that unit automatically.
const VOXELS_PER_TILE := 32
const VOXEL_UNIT := 0.1
const TILE := VOXELS_PER_TILE * VOXEL_UNIT

# Heights below are stated in voxels and converted, so they stay readable
# against the generators in assets/voxels/generators/assets.rs.
const FLOOR_VOXELS := 3 # floor_tile's slab thickness
const BELT_SURFACE_VOXELS := 10 # top of belt's running surface
const FLOOR_Y := FLOOR_VOXELS * VOXEL_UNIT
const BELT_SURFACE_Y := FLOOR_Y + BELT_SURFACE_VOXELS * VOXEL_UNIT

# How far past the chain the ISOMETRIC camera's auto-framing considers —
# kept small and deliberately separate from FLOOR_GROUND_MARGIN below, or a
# bigger floor would also zoom the default isometric view out to fit it.
const FLOOR_MARGIN_X := 2
const FLOOR_MARGIN_Z := 2
# How far the floor MESH actually extends past the chain — much larger than
# the framing margin above, so flying around in first-person doesn't reveal
# a "floating island" edge a few steps past the machines. Purely a ground
# plane; the isometric camera never zooms out to show all of it.
const FLOOR_GROUND_MARGIN_X := 24
const FLOOR_GROUND_MARGIN_Z := 24

const TICK_HZ := 30.0
## Never advance more than this many simulation ticks in one frame, and drop
## whatever backlog is left rather than paying it back later.
##
## Found while reviewing this ticket's junctions: the catch-up loop in
## `_process` was unbounded, so one slow frame — a cold shader cache on the
## first frames of a run is the reliable one — silently fast-forwarded the
## factory by however many ticks that frame was worth. Two identical
## screenshot runs of the same populate sequence photographed visibly
## different factories (one with a jammed first belt, one with it drained),
## which makes a rendered scene useless as a before/after check. It is also
## just wrong on its own terms: a rendering hitch should cost you frames, not
## silently run the simulation forward while nothing is drawn.
const MAX_TICKS_PER_FRAME := 8
const VOX_DIR := "res://voxels/"

# --- Fluid pipes (milestone 6, ARCHITECTURE.md §3.4/§9 item 6) -------------
# Which voxel model draws a pipe tile. `pipe_source` is the off-map supply
# tap (PipeSnapshot.is_source) — a different silhouette, not just a
# different colour, per UI_UX.md §7's "never encode state in colour alone."
const PIPE_ASSET := "pipe"
const PIPE_SOURCE_ASSET := "pipe_source"
## The visual-overhaul pass's fix for the pipe-to-machine connection gap: the
## one segment `fluid_snapshot_json`'s `inlet_index` names (always the run's
## machine-adjacent end, see `sim::FluidNetwork`) draws with a branch fitting
## reaching to the tile edge the machine sits against, instead of the plain
## `pipe` model whose solid header sits mid-tile and left open floor between
## it and the machine's own inlet nozzle. See `assets/voxels/generators/
## assets.rs`'s `pipe_inlet` doc for the full reasoning, including which tile
## edge and why.
const PIPE_INLET_ASSET := "pipe_inlet"
const PIPE_FLUID_ASSET := "pipe_fluid"
# Where the liquid sits inside the pipe, stated in voxels against
# assets/voxels/generators/assets.rs's PIPE_BORE_Z0 / PIPE_BORE_HEIGHT — the
# same mirroring BELT_SURFACE_VOXELS already does for the belt. The fluid
# model is deliberately ONE voxel tall (see its generator doc), so scaling it
# by `pressure * PIPE_BORE_HEIGHT_VOXELS` grows a liquid column from the bore
# floor up, with no pivot arithmetic.
const PIPE_BORE_FLOOR_VOXELS := 12
const PIPE_BORE_HEIGHT_VOXELS := 7
const PIPE_BORE_FLOOR_Y := FLOOR_Y + PIPE_BORE_FLOOR_VOXELS * VOXEL_UNIT
## Below this fill fraction the liquid body is hidden outright rather than
## drawn as a sub-voxel sliver that would z-fight the bore floor.
const FLUID_MIN_VISIBLE_PRESSURE := 0.02
## Fraction of a tile the liquid body is shortened by on the run's closed
## downstream end, mirroring the two-voxel blind flange `pipe_inlet` draws
## there (2 / 32). See `_build_fluid_network`.
const FLUID_END_CAP_TRIM := 2.0 / float(VOXELS_PER_TILE)

## Presentation-only tint per fluid id, exactly like ITEM_MODELS above: the
## fluid ids themselves come from data/recipes.ron, and a fluid with no entry
## here still renders (in a neutral pale blue) rather than vanishing. The
## voxel model is near-white so this multiplies cleanly over it.
const FLUID_TINTS := {
	"vinegar": Color(0.87, 0.75, 0.31),
}
const FLUID_TINT_FALLBACK := Color(0.60, 0.79, 0.86)

## --- Belt variants at a junction (the "Visual overhaul round 2" epic's
## ticket 3) ---------------------------------------------------------------
##
## Three models draw a belt tile, and which one a tile gets is decided from
## the simulation's own layout, never from a hardcoded "the chain runs +X":
## a tile whose NEXT tile along its stage's travel direction is covered by a
## machine footprint is that machine's hand-over tile and draws `belt_inlet`;
## a tile whose PREVIOUS tile is a machine (or the hopper) catches a
## discharge and draws `belt_outlet`; everything else is a plain `belt`.
## See `_belt_asset_for`, and `assets/voxels/generators/assets.rs`'s
## `belt_inlet` for what actually differs about the models.
##
## This is the buildable half of the project owner's "assets should look
## different depending on their orientation and what's around them": what a
## belt tile is NEXT TO is context the renderer already has on the wire.
## What a belt tile POINTS AT is not — every tile the simulation places runs
## +X and no snapshot carries a direction field, which is why the variants
## are authored for a +X run only and `_belt_asset_for` falls back to the
## plain tile for any other direction rather than drawing a wrong-facing end
## cap. See `sim_core/src/sim/world.rs`'s corner note for the full statement
## of that limitation; nothing here changes it.
const BELT_ASSET := "belt"
const BELT_INLET_ASSET := "belt_inlet"
const BELT_OUTLET_ASSET := "belt_outlet"

# --- Machines & the hopper (the "Machines improvement" milestone) ----------
## Which voxel model draws each machine KIND from `data/machines.ron`.
## Presentation-only, exactly like ITEM_MODELS below: the kind ids come from
## that data file (via `chain_layout_json`'s `machine_kind`), and a kind with
## no entry here still renders — as the generic `machine` model — rather than
## vanishing. That fallback is deliberate and load-bearing: adding a kind to
## `data/machines.ron` must never require a Godot edit before the chain will
## draw.
##
## Since the "Visual overhaul round 2" epic's ticket 2 **every kind
## `data/machines.ron` currently defines has a model of its own**, so the
## fallback is now a safety net rather than something the shipped chain
## relies on. It used to catch `bottler` and `packer`, which meant the last
## two stages both rendered as another copy of the generic housing — the
## project owner's review named exactly that ("some machines are still
## missing assets or just reuse assets (bottling machine)").
const MACHINE_MODELS := {
	"crusher": "machine_crusher",
	"mixer": "machine_mixer",
	"cooking_vat": "machine_cooking_vat",
	"bottler": "machine_bottler",
	"packer": "machine_packer",
	"aging_rack": "machine_aging_rack",
	# The chain's exit (the delivery-dock ticket). It is not a `chain_layout_json`
	# stage — nothing in `data/recipes.ron` names it — so `_build_chain` never
	# looks it up; `_build_dock` and the build menu's `_shop_asset_for` both do,
	# and they find it here rather than in a table of their own.
	"dock": "machine_dock",
}
const MACHINE_MODEL_FALLBACK := "machine"

## Which machine kinds draw a live PROGRESS GAUGE beside their model, and
## which asset that gauge's moving bar is (ARCHITECTURE.md §9 milestone 7).
##
## Keyed on kind, like MACHINE_MODELS, and deliberately sparse: a gauge is
## not a thing every machine should have. The crusher's whole process is 60
## ticks — one second — so a bar tracking it would be a flicker, and five
## flickering bars along the line would be noise rather than information.
## The aging rack's is 2400 ticks (data/recipes.ron), forty seconds, which
## is long enough that the interesting question about it is not "is it
## running" (its status lamp already answers that) but "how far through is
## it" — and that question deserves an answer a player gets by GLANCING,
## not by parking a cursor on the machine and reading a tooltip. That is the
## whole reason this mapping exists: the milestone is about a process at a
## qualitatively different timescale, and a timescale you have to hover to
## perceive has not really been presented.
##
## A kind with no entry here simply has no gauge — the same deliberate
## fall-through MACHINE_MODELS documents, so pricing or shipping a new kind
## never requires a Godot edit first.
const MACHINE_PROGRESS_GAUGES := {
	"aging_rack": "aging_rack_gauge",
}

## The gauge's slot geometry, mirroring assets/voxels/generators/assets.rs's
## GAUGE_Z0 / GAUGE_HEIGHT — stated in voxels, the same arrangement (and the
## same change-one-change-both warning) the pipe bore, the hopper fill and
## the tank fill already carry.
const MACHINE_GAUGE_FLOOR_VOXELS := 11
const MACHINE_GAUGE_HEIGHT_VOXELS := 21
const MACHINE_GAUGE_FLOOR_Y := FLOOR_Y + MACHINE_GAUGE_FLOOR_VOXELS * VOXEL_UNIT
## Below this fraction the bar is hidden outright rather than drawn as a
## sub-voxel sliver — the same call FLUID_MIN_VISIBLE_PRESSURE,
## HOPPER_MIN_VISIBLE_FILL and FLUID_TANK_MIN_VISIBLE_FILL all make. An idle
## rack therefore shows an empty black slot, which is a SHAPE difference
## rather than a colour one (UI_UX.md §7).
const MACHINE_GAUGE_MIN_VISIBLE := 0.01

## The chain's raw-ingredient hopper and the bulk product visible inside it.
## The fill body is the solid counterpart of PIPE_FLUID_ASSET and is driven
## exactly the same way — one voxel tall, scaled vertically to a live fill
## fraction every frame (see `_update_hopper`). These two constants mirror
## assets/voxels/generators/assets.rs's HOPPER_FILL_Z0 / HOPPER_FILL_HEIGHT,
## stated in voxels like the PIPE_BORE_* pair above; change one, change both.
const HOPPER_ASSET := "hopper"
const HOPPER_FILL_ASSET := "hopper_fill"
const HOPPER_FILL_FLOOR_VOXELS := 21
const HOPPER_FILL_HEIGHT_VOXELS := 9
const HOPPER_FILL_FLOOR_Y := FLOOR_Y + HOPPER_FILL_FLOOR_VOXELS * VOXEL_UNIT
## Below this fill fraction the product body is hidden outright rather than
## drawn as a sub-voxel sliver — the same call FLUID_MIN_VISIBLE_PRESSURE makes.
const HOPPER_MIN_VISIBLE_FILL := 0.02

## The pipe run's fluid tank and the liquid standing in it (the "Visual
## overhaul round 2" epic — ticket 1 made the tank real in simulation, this
## draws it). Deliberately the exact shape the hopper pair above has: a body
## asset plus a one-voxel-tall fill body scaled to a live fraction every
## frame, because the tank IS the fluid twin of the hopper on the wire
## (`fluid_tank_snapshot_json` is field-for-field `hopper_snapshot_json`), so
## it should be the same code path here too rather than a third mechanism.
## These two constants mirror assets/voxels/generators/assets.rs's
## TANK_FILL_Z0 / TANK_FILL_HEIGHT; change one, change both.
const FLUID_TANK_ASSET := "fluid_tank"
const FLUID_TANK_FILL_ASSET := "fluid_tank_fill"
const FLUID_TANK_FILL_FLOOR_VOXELS := 8
const FLUID_TANK_FILL_HEIGHT_VOXELS := 18
const FLUID_TANK_FILL_FLOOR_Y := FLOOR_Y + FLUID_TANK_FILL_FLOOR_VOXELS * VOXEL_UNIT
## Below this fill fraction the liquid column is hidden outright rather than
## drawn as a sub-voxel sliver — the same call FLUID_MIN_VISIBLE_PRESSURE and
## HOPPER_MIN_VISIBLE_FILL both make.
const FLUID_TANK_MIN_VISIBLE_FILL := 0.02

## The chain's delivery dock (the delivery-dock ticket) and the goods
## standing on it.
##
## **There is deliberately no fill gauge here**, unlike the hopper and the
## tank above, and that is a decision rather than an omission. The dock is a
## pass-through: `sim_core::sim::dock::Dock::ship_into` empties its buffer
## into the economy's shared delivery pool in the same tick it arrived, so
## `dock_snapshot_json`'s `contents` is always empty between ticks and
## `DockSnapshot` carries no `fill` field at all. A bar driven from it would
## read zero forever — a gauge that lies about the mechanism, which is
## exactly what `FluidTank` refused to build when it chose constant head over
## its own fill fraction.
##
## What IS real, and what the goods on the deck are drawn from, is
## `economy_snapshot_json`'s `delivery_stock` — the one shared "what's on the
## dock" figure every contract settles against, and the number the clipboard
## panel already labels ON THE DOCK. `sim::DockSnapshot`'s own doc says to
## draw the stack from it, and that is what `_update_dock_stock` does: it
## stands the item's OWN model (through ITEM_MODELS, so a case looks like the
## case that arrived) on the deck, one per unit, capped at DOCK_STOCK_SLOTS.
const DOCK_ASSET := "machine_dock"
## Top of the dock's deck, mirroring assets/voxels/generators/assets.rs's
## DOCK_DECK_Z — which is BELT_SURFACE_VOXELS - 1 there, i.e. the same
## running height as the belt that feeds it. Change one, change both.
const DOCK_DECK_Y := BELT_SURFACE_Y
## Where each waiting case stands, in the dock model's own voxel coordinates
## (x, y) measured from its footprint's centre: side by side along the deck's
## NEAR edge, which is where staged goods actually go and — the reason these
## two numbers rather than any others — the one part of the deck a case can
## stand on without hiding the building it is standing on.
##
## Two slots, not one per unit the contract board can ask for. The deck is one
## tile and a case is a third of it, so a rendered four-slot version buried
## the transfer housing, the despatch door and the canopy under a wall of
## cardboard — a dock you cannot see is not a better indicator than a dock
## with two cases on it. This is a glance-readable "goods are waiting to be
## sold", not a readout: the exact figure is on the contract clipboard (ON
## THE DOCK) and in the dock's own hover tooltip, both of which report the
## same pool without a cap.
const DOCK_STOCK_OFFSETS := [
	Vector2(1.0, -9.0),
	Vector2(11.0, -9.0),
]
const DOCK_STOCK_SLOTS := 2

## Presentation-only tint per raw-ingredient item id, the same shape as
## FLUID_TINTS: *which* item a hopper holds is simulation data (the first
## recipe's first input, see sim_core/src/sim/hopper.rs), so the model's
## voxels are near-white and this multiplies over them.
const HOPPER_FILL_TINTS := {
	"tomato": Color(0.76, 0.17, 0.13),
}
const HOPPER_FILL_TINT_FALLBACK := Color(0.72, 0.66, 0.56)

## Which voxel model represents each item on a belt. Presentation-only
## mapping: the item ids themselves come from data/recipes.ron, and an item
## with no entry here still renders (as a shipping box) rather than vanishing.
##
## Keep these keys matched to `data/recipes.ron`'s actual item ids: the
## fallback means a drifted key fails *quietly*, as bulk product rendering as
## a sealed cardboard box. That is exactly what happened when the "Machines
## improvement" milestone's ticket 2 replaced `tomato_sauce` with
## `tomato_paste` + `ketchup_mix` — the mapping still named the old id, and
## two whole stages of the chain shipped boxes down the belt until this
## ticket's rendered-scene review caught it.
const ITEM_MODELS := {
	"tomato": "item_tomato",
	"tomato_paste": "item_paste_pail",
	"ketchup_mix": "item_mix_pail",
	"ketchup": "item_ketchup_pail",
	"bottled_ketchup": "item_bottle",
	"boxed_ketchup": "item_box",
}
const ITEM_MODEL_FALLBACK := "item_box"

## Which voxel model's icon stands for each *buildable kind* in the build
## menu (the "Shop & Inventory" epic's ticket 2). This is the kind-id twin of
## ITEM_MODELS above — `data/shop.ron`'s ids are buildable kinds, not item
## ids, so they can't go through that table — and it only needs to name the
## kinds MACHINE_MODELS doesn't already cover: the two pieces of
## infrastructure and the two buffers, each of which happens to share its
## asset's own name. `_shop_asset_for` consults this first, then
## MACHINE_MODELS, then MACHINE_MODEL_FALLBACK, so a kind added to
## `data/shop.ron` still shows *something* rather than vanishing from the
## menu — the same deliberate fallback MACHINE_MODELS documents.
const SHOP_MODELS := {
	"belt": "belt",
	"pipe": "pipe",
	"hopper": "hopper",
	"fluid_tank": "fluid_tank",
}

## UI icons (UI_UX.md §5): small, transparent-background renders of the same
## voxel models above, generated offline by
## assets/voxels/generators/icons.rs (`cargo run -p voxgen --bin voxicons`,
## or `scripts/build-voxels.sh`) and staged here by `cargo xtask`, same
## treatment as `res://voxels/` itself. See `_icon_for` below.
const ICON_DIR := "res://voxels/icons/"

var sim: SimBridge

var _tick_accum := 0.0
var _stage_order: Array[String] = [] # recipe ids, in chain order
var _machine_tiles: Dictionary = {} # recipe id -> Vector2i machine ORIGIN tile (min-X/min-Y corner)
var _machine_footprints: Dictionary = {} # recipe id -> Vector2i(width, height) in tiles, from data/machines.ron
var _last_machines: Dictionary = {} # recipe id -> last machine snapshot dict
var _machine_lamps: Dictionary = {} # recipe id -> MeshInstance3D (emissive mesh)
var _machine_gauges: Dictionary = {} # recipe id -> Node3D, the live progress bar (see MACHINE_PROGRESS_GAUGES)
var _machine_running: Dictionary = {} # recipe id -> bool, to avoid redundant swaps
var _item_nodes: Dictionary = {} # pooled item nodes, keyed per belt slot
var _chain_tile_width := 5

# --- Fluid network (milestone 6) -------------------------------------------
# Geometry is built once from fluid_snapshot_json's `segments` (see
# _build_fluid_network for why that call, not chunk snapshots, is the right
# source); levels are re-read from the same call every frame.
var _pipe_tiles: Dictionary = {} # Vector2i tile -> segment index, for hover lookups
var _pipe_fluid_nodes: Array[Node3D] = [] # one liquid body per segment, indexed alike
var _fluid_material: StandardMaterial3D
var _last_fluid: Dictionary = {} # last fluid snapshot; {} when this world has no pipe run

# --- The chain's hopper (ticket 3's building, rendered here) ---------------
# Geometry is built once from hopper_snapshot_json; the fill level is re-read
# from the same call every frame, the same poll-don't-dirty-track trade
# _update_fluid_network makes.
var _hopper_tile := Vector2i.ZERO # footprint origin (min-X/min-Y corner)
var _hopper_footprint := Vector2i.ONE
var _hopper_fill_node: Node3D
## The hopper's one status lamp (see `hopper()`'s doc), swapped between the
## same idle/running materials a machine's lamp uses — the visual-overhaul
## pass's answer to "does filling the hopper give feedback beyond its level
## slowly rising": the lamp now actually reports "holds stock"/"empty" the
## same way a machine's lamp reports running/idle, rather than sitting a
## fixed colour regardless of state.
var _hopper_lamp: MeshInstance3D
var _hopper_has_stock: bool = false
var _last_hopper: Dictionary = {} # last hopper snapshot, for the hover tooltip

# --- The pipe run's fluid tank (ticket 1's building, rendered here) --------
# Geometry is built once from fluid_tank_snapshot_json; the level is re-read
# from the same call every frame. Polling is not optional here the way it is
# for the hopper: the tank is drained by the same equalization pass the pipe
# run is, and that pass deliberately does NOT dirty its chunk, so a
# chunk-driven renderer would never learn the level fell (see
# SimBridge::fluid_tank_snapshot_json's own doc).
var _fluid_tank_tile := Vector2i.ZERO # footprint origin (min-X/min-Y corner)
var _fluid_tank_footprint := Vector2i.ONE
var _fluid_tank_fill_node: Node3D
var _fluid_tank_lamp: MeshInstance3D
var _fluid_tank_has_stock: bool = false
var _last_fluid_tank: Dictionary = {} # last tank snapshot; {} when this world has no pipe run

# --- The chain's delivery dock (the delivery-dock ticket) ------------------
# Geometry (the dock, and the one belt that runs into it) is built once from
# dock_snapshot_json; the goods standing on the deck are re-read every frame
# from economy_snapshot_json — see DOCK_ASSET's doc for why those are two
# different calls rather than one.
var _dock_tile := Vector2i.ZERO # footprint origin (min-X/min-Y corner)
var _dock_footprint := Vector2i.ONE
var _dock_stock_nodes: Array[Node3D] = []
var _dock_lamp: MeshInstance3D
var _dock_has_stock: bool = false
var _last_dock: Dictionary = {} # last dock snapshot, for the hover tooltip
var _last_dock_stock: Array = [] # economy_snapshot_json's delivery_stock, likewise

var _class_materials: Dictionary = {} # "matte"/"metal"/"emissive" -> StandardMaterial3D
var _lamp_idle: StandardMaterial3D
var _lamp_running: StandardMaterial3D
var _asset_classes: Dictionary = {} # asset name -> Array of class names

var _help_label: Label

# --- Contract clipboard (UI_UX.md §5) --------------------------------------
# The money/contract readout used to be one plain Label folded into the
# generic dark HUD panel above. It now lives in its own diegetic "clipboard"
# panel (a distinct node, per that ticket) so ticket A's hover-tooltip work
# can freely reshape or remove the machine-line panel above without ever
# touching this one.
#
# It shows EVERY active contract, not one. `economy_snapshot_json` reports an
# array of contracts (each with its own mix, cadence and payout, all drawing
# from one shared delivery pool — see sim_core/src/sim/economy.rs), and
# GAMEPLAY.md §6's whole design pressure is that several concurrent orders
# compete for one factory's throughput. That pressure only exists for the
# player if all of them are actually on screen at once, so the clipboard is
# now a board of dockets: ONE sheet of paper (the panel, its steel clip bar
# unchanged) carrying the shared money figure and the shared dock stock, then
# one printed docket card per contract below it.
var _contract_panel: PanelContainer
